LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Material Request, field by field", "14", "<p>Every purchase your firm makes began as a NEED somewhere — a shelf running low, a branch running out, a project needing kit. The <b>Material Request</b> is where need becomes a document, and this module is about why that step exists and how to run it well. First, the anatomy.</p><ul><li><b>Purpose</b> — the request's TYPE, the single most consequential field: <b>Purchase</b> (buy this from outside), <b>Material Transfer</b> (move it between our own warehouses), <b>Material Issue</b> (consume it internally — stationery to admin, spares to maintenance). The purpose decides what the request BECOMES downstream (Chapter 3), so choosing it correctly is choosing the right machinery.</li><li><b>Requested By and the requesting context</b> — who needs this. The requester is the document's voice; procurement will route questions to them, and the audit trail starts with them.</li><li><b>Required By (schedule date)</b> — when the need bites: header-level, and refinable <b>per row</b> for staggered needs. This date is the request's honesty test — Chapter 6 convicts the everything-urgent culture — and it is what procurement plans backward from: required-by minus lead time equals order-by, which is why Buying 2's honest lead times matter here.</li><li><b>The items grid</b> — item, quantity, <b>UOM</b> (the trade unit with its conversion — Buying 2's arithmetic applies: a branch requesting 10 Carton-12 is requesting 120 units, and both readings must agree), and per row the <b>target warehouse</b> — where the goods should END UP. For a purchase, the receiving warehouse; for a transfer, the destination branch store. The warehouse on the row is what makes multi-location demand legible.</li><li><b>Status machinery</b> — Draft, then Submitted (Pending); as downstream documents are made FROM it, the request tracks <b>ordered percentage</b> per row — Partially Ordered, then Ordered (or Transferred / Issued per purpose); Stopped ends it deliberately; Cancelled withdraws it. The create-from spine you know from the selling cycle runs here too: only documents made FROM the request count against it.</li></ul><p><b>Draft, check, submit.</b> The MR is the cheapest document in the whole procurement cycle to get right — nothing has been promised, bought, or moved. Thirty seconds in Draft (right purpose? right warehouse? quantities in a sane UOM? honest date?) saves every downstream document from inheriting a malformed need. Submission makes it visible: the request enters procurement's queue, and Chapter 2 explains why that visibility is the entire point.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Why a demand layer exists", "13", "<p>A fair question from every impatient client: why not just raise purchase orders directly? The answer is the module's thesis, and a consultant must be able to argue it in one minute.</p><p><b>Separation of need from commitment.</b> The Material Request says WE NEED THIS; the Purchase Order says WE ARE BUYING THIS FROM YOU AT THIS PRICE. Different statements, different authority. ANYONE appropriate can raise a request — the storekeeper who sees the shelf, the branch manager who knows their weekend, the project lead with a kit list — because stating a need requires knowing the need. COMMITTING money requires knowing the suppliers, the rates, the budget, and the consolidation picture — which is procurement's job. The MR layer lets the whole organisation contribute demand without distributing the chequebook.</p><p><b>What the separation buys, concretely:</b></p><ul><li><b>Maverick buying dies.</b> Where policy says purchases begin as requests, the PO raised from nowhere — the friend-of-a-staff-member supplier, the branch buying locally at retail, the duplicate order two people phoned in — becomes visible as a policy exception rather than invisible as normal noise. The audit question WHO NEEDED THIS has a documented answer for every naira spent.</li><li><b>Consolidation becomes possible.</b> Five branches each need paracetamol; five direct POs would buy five times at five moments. Five REQUESTS sitting in one queue become ONE consolidated order at pallet economics (Chapter 7's mechanics) — and consolidated spend is negotiating leverage (Buying 1, Chapter 2's argument, now operational).</li><li><b>Demand data exists.</b> Requests are a record of what the organisation NEEDED, distinct from what it bought — the unmet-demand read (requested but never fulfilled, and why) that pure PO history cannot give.</li><li><b>The queue is manageable.</b> One inbox, oldest-and-most-urgent first, with statuses — versus needs living in WhatsApp messages, corridor conversations, and sticky notes, fulfilled in order of who shouts loudest.</li></ul><p><b>Proportionality — the consultant's judgment.</b> Not every firm needs every purchase through MRs: an 11-person firm's MD buying a laptop can go straight to PO without ceremony; a 30-branch chain's replenishment absolutely cannot. The implementation conversation sets the policy: which purchase categories REQUIRE the request layer (stock replenishment: always; branch demand: always; ad-hoc small services: often not), and the system's role is making the required path the easy path. The layer is governance, and governance is sized to what it governs.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The three purposes in practice", "13", "<p>The purpose field routes the request into one of three different machines. Choosing correctly is this chapter; the wrong choice is Chapter 9's most common ticket.</p><p><b>Purchase — buy it from outside.</b> The need cannot be met from our own stock anywhere: the request's destiny is a supplier. Downstream it becomes an RFQ (sourcing new — Buying 4) or a Purchase Order directly (known supplier, agreed rates — Buying 5), and the request tracks its ordered percentage as POs are made from it. The target warehouse on each row is where the goods will be RECEIVED — which the PO inherits, which the receipt executes (Buying 6).</p><p><b>Material Transfer — move it between our warehouses.</b> The need exists at one location while the stock exists at another: the Ibadan branch is out of what the Lagos central store holds in pallets. The request's destiny is a <b>stock transfer</b>, not a purchase — no supplier, no money, just internal movement with a paper trail: source warehouse, target warehouse, the transfer document executing it, the request tracking transferred percentage. This is the machinery of <b>branch replenishment</b>, the multi-location lifeblood (Chapter 5 gives it a full chapter), and buying externally what your own central store already holds is the expensive version of choosing the wrong purpose.</p><p><b>Material Issue — consume it internally.</b> The need is consumption, not movement-for-sale: stationery to the admin office, cleaning supplies to facilities, spare parts to maintenance. The request's destiny is an issue document that writes stock OUT to an expense context — the goods leave the ledger deliberately, attributed, instead of evaporating. Internal consumption without issues is how consumables shrinkage hides: the stock count that never reconciles because usage was real but unrecorded.</p><p><b>The choosing test, one line each.</b> Must it come from outside? — Purchase. Do we hold it elsewhere? — Transfer. Are we using it up ourselves? — Issue. The test is asked in that ORDER, because the cheapest fulfilment is the one you already own: check internal availability before buying is a one-look habit (the projected-quantity view across warehouses) that pays for the whole request layer by itself in avoided duplicate stock.</p><p><b>Mixed needs split cleanly.</b> A branch's monthly list may contain all three kinds — resale stock (transfer from central), a local-only item central never carries (purchase), cleaning materials (issue). Three purposes, three requests — not one request with a blended identity that no downstream machine can execute. The requester learns the split once; the templates (a branch's standard monthly request per purpose) make it effortless thereafter.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Auto-replenishment: the trigger fires here", "13", "<p>Buying 2, Chapter 5 set the planning fields — reorder level, reorder quantity, lead time, safety stock. This chapter is where they come alive: the system watching projected stock and raising Material Requests <b>automatically</b> when triggers are crossed.</p><p><b>The mechanics.</b> With reorder levels set (per item, per warehouse where set), a scheduled evaluation compares <b>projected quantity</b> — actual minus reserved plus incoming, the same arithmetic the whole trading position runs on — against each trigger. Crossing it raises a Material Request for the reorder quantity, purpose per the item's replenishment design (Purchase for centrally-bought stock; Transfer where a branch replenishes from central). The request lands in the same queue as human ones, marked by its origin, and flows through the same downstream machinery. Replenishment stops depending on somebody noticing.</p><p><b>Auto is not autopilot.</b> The auto-MR is a PROPOSAL computed from the fields' honesty, and the review posture matters: procurement scans the auto queue with human context the fields lack — the promotion that will spike demand next month, the supplier holiday, the discontinuation decision the master doesn't know yet. The right rhythm is review-and-release, not blind conversion and not (the opposite failure) letting auto requests rot unactioned because <i>the system raised them, not a person</i>. An auto request IS a person speaking — the person who set the level — at one remove.</p><p><b>Tuning, not disabling.</b> The predictable complaint after go-live: <i>the system raises too many requests</i> — or too few, discovered as stockouts. Both are the FIELDS talking, not the machinery failing: levels set high in caution flood the queue; dishonest lead times fire triggers too late; dead items with live levels request themselves forever. The fix is Buying 2's quarterly exception review (the stockouts and the never-turns), item by item — and the discipline to resist the lazy fix of switching auto-replenishment off, which returns the firm to shelf-watching by memory and calls it peace. A noisy trigger is mistuned; a disabled trigger is blind.</p><p><b>What auto-replenishment changes commercially.</b> Stock-outs on running lines become a FIELDS problem with a named fix instead of a recurring emergency; buying smooths into planned consolidations instead of panic singles (the freight and price difference is real money); and the procurement team's attention moves up a level — from remembering what to buy, to judging what the system proposes and negotiating how it is bought. That attention shift is the entire return on the master-data work, and it is why the fields chapter and this chapter are one system taught in two places.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Branch demand & the transfer flow", "13", "<p>For multi-location trade — your pharmacy chains, your supermarket clients, your own multi-branch reality — the Material Request is the <b>branch's voice</b>, and the transfer flow is how the network breathes. This chapter runs it end to end.</p><p><b>The shape of the network.</b> The common Nigerian pattern: central procurement buys in bulk into a main store (pallet economics, consolidated negotiation — the whole Buying 1-2 architecture), and branches replenish FROM central by transfer. Branches rarely buy externally; when they do, it is exceptional and visible. The request layer enforces the shape: branch need enters as <b>Material Transfer requests</b> targeting the branch warehouse, sourced from central — never as branch-level purchases that fragment the buying power the shape exists to create.</p><p><b>The branch request, well made.</b> The branch manager (or the branch's own auto-triggers — reorder levels per branch warehouse, Chapter 4) raises the transfer request: items in trade UOMs, quantities honest to the branch's velocity, required-by aligned to the delivery run. The craft points: request to the RUN (if trucks go Tuesdays, Thursday's request is next Tuesday's — the required-by says so honestly); request to CAPACITY (the branch that orders three months of bulk to feel safe is warehousing central's stock badly); and request ONCE (the duplicate-demand pattern — the manager requests, the auto-trigger also fires — is handled by procurement's consolidation eye, Chapter 7, but branches learn to check their own open requests first).</p><p><b>Central's side of the flow.</b> The transfer queue, grouped by branch and run: procurement (or the warehouse) reviews, adjusts against central's own stock position (a branch's 40 requested cartons against central's 25 available is a partial transfer plus a purchase request for the gap — the request layer handles the split honestly), and executes via the stock-transfer documents that move the goods with full paper: what left central, what should arrive at the branch, and the receiving confirmation closing the loop. The request tracks its transferred percentage throughout — the branch can SEE their request moving without a phone call, which at 30 branches is a hundred phone calls a week that stop happening.</p><p><b>When transfer becomes purchase.</b> Central lacks it entirely: the branch's transfer request surfaces the gap, and procurement raises the PURCHASE request (or converts the demand) for central to buy — the branch's voice heard, the buying still consolidated. The anti-pattern this replaces: the branch, tired of waiting, buys locally at retail — solving Tuesday by bleeding margin, and invisible until someone audits branch petty cash. Where the flow is fast and visible, the anti-pattern starves.</p><p><b>The demand picture nobody had.</b> Six months of branch requests IS the network's demand map: which branches grow, which items travel, which requests go chronically unmet (central under-buying — a planning-fields conversation). The map exists as a by-product of the discipline; firms without the layer pay consultants to reconstruct it from receipts.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Approval & governance: running the queue", "13", "<p>The request layer is only as good as the queue's governance: who approves what, how honestly dates are set, and how requests die when they should. This chapter is the operating discipline.</p><p><b>Approval, sized to stakes.</b> The workflow question is WHO MUST AGREE before a request becomes procurement's work: routine replenishment inside set levels can auto-proceed (the level WAS the approval, pre-granted — the same logic as rule-given discounts in Selling 6); unusual quantities, new items, or value thresholds route to a manager; capital-ish purchases route higher. Where formal workflow states are configured, the system enforces the route; where they are not, the queue review IS the approval gate — either way the policy exists, is written, and is sized to the firm (an 11-person company needs a threshold, not a committee).</p><p><b>Required-by honesty — the date culture.</b> The everything-urgent disease: when every request says NOW, the date carries no information, procurement triages by volume of shouting, and genuinely urgent needs drown. The cure is cultural and enforced gently: dates are planning inputs (required-by minus lead time equals order-by — a date inside the lead time is already late at birth, and the requester should hear that arithmetic kindly, once); chronic date-fiction requesters get the conversation; and TRUE urgency, clearly flagged and occasionally real, gets honoured fast — which is only possible because it is rare. A queue with honest dates sorts itself.</p><p><b>Rejecting well.</b> Not every need should be met: budget says no, stock says use-what-we-have, the item is being discontinued. The discipline is selling's lost-reason lesson wearing procurement clothes: the rejection carries a REASON, returned to the requester — because a request that vanishes into silence teaches the organisation to bypass the layer, while a request answered with <i>no, because, and here is the alternative</i> teaches it to trust the queue. Rejection reasons aggregate into their own read: what the organisation keeps asking for and not getting is either discipline working or a signal being ignored.</p><p><b>Stopping and amending.</b> A need that evaporates (the project cancelled, the demand misjudged) gets its request <b>Stopped</b> — deliberately ended, remaining quantities released from the pipeline, history intact — the Close discipline from Selling 4, demand-side. A need that CHANGED amends through the proper doorway rather than a second overlapping request. The monthly sweep mirrors the order sweep: open requests past their required-by with no action — each one actioned, stopped, or re-dated honestly.</p><p><b>The queue rhythm.</b> Procurement's inbox, worked daily: new requests reviewed (purpose right? internal availability checked? consolidation candidates flagged?), the aging tail swept, auto-requests judged (Chapter 4's posture). Fifteen minutes a day keeps the layer trusted; a week's neglect teaches the whole firm to phone instead.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — From request to action: the downstream paths", "13", "<p>A submitted request is potential energy; this chapter is the release — the paths from need to commitment, and the tracking that keeps every request honest about its own fate.</p><p><b>Path one: request → Purchase Order (the known route).</b> The item has an established supplier and an agreed rate (the supplier-scoped Item Price of Buying 2): procurement creates the PO <b>from the request</b> — items, quantities, target warehouse carrying over; the supplier and rate arriving from the masters; the request's ordered-percentage advancing. The create-from law, third appearance in this track: only a PO made FROM the request counts against it — a PO typed from scratch fulfils the shelf while orphaning the demand record, and the request ages as unmet forever (Chapter 9's ticket).</p><p><b>Path two: request → RFQ → comparison → PO (the sourcing route).</b> New item, new volume tier, or a periodic market test: the request feeds a Request for Quotation to candidate suppliers, quotes come back as Supplier Quotations, the comparison decides, the PO follows to the winner — Buying 4 owns the whole machinery; here, know that the request is its trigger and its traceability spine: the eventual PO chains back through the sourcing decision to the original need.</p><p><b>Consolidation — the queue's superpower.</b> Multiple requests, one purchase: five branch requests for paracetamol plus the central auto-trigger become ONE Emzor PO at pallet economics, each request's rows drawn into the consolidated order, each tracking ITS ordered percentage against its own quantities. The mechanics support it; the craft is procurement's daily scan for consolidation candidates (same item, same supplier, compatible dates) — the scan is where the buying-power thesis of Chapter 2 becomes cash, order by order.</p><p><b>Partial fulfilment.</b> A 100-carton request met by a 60-carton order (budget, availability) leaves the request <b>Partially Ordered</b>, the 40 visible and pending — honest, trackable, and either completed later or Stopped deliberately with the requester told. What partiality never becomes: silent — the drift where remainders are neither ordered nor stopped nor explained is how the layer loses the organisation's trust.</p><p><b>Transfer and issue paths.</b> The same create-from discipline: transfer requests fulfilled by stock-transfer documents made from them; issue requests by issue documents likewise — percentages tracking, loops closing, the branch or department seeing their request's state without a call.</p><p><b>Reading a request's fate.</b> Any request answers, on its own face: what was asked, when it was needed, what has been ordered/transferred/issued against it, what remains, and by which documents — the chain of custody from shelf-gap to goods, reconstructible by anyone, forever. That legibility is what the whole module has been building.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: a month of demand at HealthTrade", "16", "<p>One month, every chapter working. HealthTrade — supply base built (Procurement 1), item architecture set (Procurement 2) — now runs its demand layer. Read actively; each step names its chapter.</p><p><b>Week 1 — the auto-trigger fires (Ch. 4).</b> PARA-500's central-store projected quantity crosses its reorder level (consumption ran hot). The system raises a Purchase-type Material Request for the reorder quantity — 200 Carton-12 — target warehouse Central, origin marked auto. Procurement's morning review confirms it with context the trigger lacked: a radio promo starts next month, so the release is nudged to 260 cartons. Judged, not rubber-stamped.</p><p><b>Week 1, same morning — the branch voice (Ch. 3, 5).</b> Ibadan's manager submits the weekly Transfer request to the Tuesday run: 40 Carton-12 PARA-500 plus twelve other lines, target warehouse Ibadan Store, required-by Tuesday. Central holds only 25 PARA cartons: the transfer executes 25 (partial, tracked), and the 15-carton gap folds into the purchase demand — the branch's voice heard, the buying still central (Ch. 5's split, exactly).</p><p><b>Week 2 — the consolidated order (Ch. 7).</b> The purchase queue now holds: the auto request (260), the Ibadan gap (15), and Abeokuta's smaller flag (25). One Emzor PO for 300 Carton-12 at the pallet rate, created from the requests — each tracking its own ordered percentage, the negotiation carrying the consolidated number (Ch. 2's leverage, cashed).</p><p><b>Week 2 — the wrong-purpose catch (Ch. 3, 6).</b> A new branch assistant raises a PURCHASE request for shelving units that central's facilities store already holds. The queue review catches it — internal availability first — and the request is rejected with the reason and the redirect: <i>re-raise as Transfer from Facilities; stock exists</i>. The assistant learns the choosing test; the layer teaches through its answers.</p><p><b>Week 3 — the project request and the threshold (Ch. 6).</b> The ops lead requests a branded chiller for a new counter — above the value threshold, so the request routes to the MD's approval; approved with a note, it feeds an RFQ to two suppliers (Buying 4's machinery warming up). Required-by is honest: six weeks, matching the sourcing time.</p><p><b>Week 4 — the sweep (Ch. 6).</b> The monthly queue sweep finds one stale request: cleaning supplies asked three weeks ago, purpose Issue, never actioned because facilities went on leave. Actioned same day, and the miss noted — the queue's trust is repaired by the sweep existing.</p><p><b>Month-end reads (Ch. 2, 5).</b> The demand record now answers: requested-vs-fulfilled by branch (Ibadan's chronic PARA gap flags a reorder-level review at central — the planning-fields conversation), rejection reasons (one wrong-purpose, one budget-no), and consolidation captured (300 cartons bought as one order instead of three). Zero purchases happened without a documented need; zero branch retail-buying occurred because the flow was faster than the workaround. The layer paid its rent in month one.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & support patterns", "13", "<p>The demand layer's scar tissue: the tickets, the diseases, the fixes — and the module's law.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — \"POs keep appearing that never had requests.\"</b> Disease: maverick buying where policy requires the layer (Ch. 2) — visible now precisely BECAUSE the layer exists. Fix: the policy restated with its category boundaries, the exceptions reviewed as exceptions; where a category genuinely doesn't need requests, say so in the policy rather than eroding it silently.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — \"We bought externally what central had in pallets.\"</b> Disease: wrong purpose — Purchase chosen where Transfer was true (Ch. 3), usually because internal availability was never checked. Fix: the choosing test taught in order (outside? elsewhere? ourselves?), and the one-look projected-quantity habit before any purchase request proceeds.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — \"The system floods us with requests / never warns us.\"</b> Disease: the fields talking (Ch. 4) — levels too cautious or lead times dishonest. Fix: tune via the quarterly exception review; never the lazy fix of disabling auto-replenishment, which trades a noisy trigger for a blind one.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — \"Every request says urgent.\"</b> Disease: date fiction (Ch. 6) — the field carries no information, so triage runs on volume. Fix: the arithmetic conversation (required-by minus lead time), chronic offenders coached, true urgency honoured fast because it is rare.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — \"Requests disappear into a black hole.\"</b> Disease: the unowned queue (Ch. 6) — needs submitted and never answered teach the firm to phone and bypass. Fix: the daily fifteen-minute rhythm with a named owner, the monthly sweep, and rejections that carry reasons — the queue's trust is rebuilt by its response time.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — \"The same demand was bought twice.\"</b> Disease: duplicate requests un-consolidated (Ch. 5, 7) — the branch manager AND the auto-trigger both spoke, and two POs answered. Fix: the consolidation scan (same item, same supplier, compatible dates) as a daily habit; requesters taught to check their open requests; and the create-from discipline so every PO's demand ancestry is visible at a glance.</p><p><b>Pattern 7 — \"The request has aged as unmet, but the goods arrived weeks ago.\"</b> Disease: the from-scratch PO (Ch. 7) — the shelf was fulfilled while the demand record was orphaned; the request still says pending, and the tracking layer now lies. Fix: reconcile the orphan deliberately, then the create-from reflex — the third module in this track to teach it, because it is the same law at every joint of the chain.</p><p><b>The law.</b> Procurement 1: purchase documents are downstream of supplier masters. Procurement 2: cost truth begins at the item. This module adds the third: <b>the request layer separates need from commitment — and the separation only governs if the join is governed.</b> Anyone may speak a need; procurement alone converts it to money; and the queue between them — honest dates, judged auto-triggers, reasons on rejections, consolidation at the join, create-from at every step — is where a firm's buying becomes deliberate. A trading company that masters this module never again answers the auditor's WHO NEEDED THIS with silence, and never again buys at retail what it owned in pallets.</p>"),
]

